{{smaller block|us no hurt, as we have not ''touched'' thee. {{right|Gen. xxvi. 29.}}}}

{{smaller block|No loss shall ''touch'' her by my company.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., iii. 1. 180.}}}}

{{smaller block|No temporal Law could ''touch'' the innocence of thir lives. {{right|''Milton'', Eikonoklastes, xvii.}}}}

'''9†.''' To test by contact, as in trying gold with a touchstone; hence, to test; try; probe.

{{smaller block|Wherein I mean to ''touch'' your love indeed.
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, iii. 3. 81.}}}}

{{smaller block|There 's no judgment
Goes true upon man's outside, there 's the mischief;
He must be ''touch'd'' and tried, for gold or dross.
{{right|''Fletcher'' (''and another'' ?), Nice Valour, iv. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|Words so debased and hard, no stone
Was hard enough to ''touch'' them on.
{{right|''S. Butler'', Hudibras, I. i. 112.}}}}

'''10.''' To touch upon; handle or treat lightly or cursorily; refer or allude to, as in passing.

{{smaller block|''Touch'' you the sourest points with sweetest terms.
{{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., ii. 2. 24.}}}}

{{smaller block|We glanced from theme to theme,
Discussed the books to love or hate,
Or ''touch'd'' the changes of the state.
{{right|''Tennyson'', In Memoriam, lxxxix.}}}}

'''11†.''' To communicate; speak; tell; rehearse; relate; mention.

{{smaller block|Bot I ''touche'' thaym to the a lyttill for thou sulde by this littill vndirstande the more.
{{right|''Hampole'', Prose Treatises (E. E. T. S.), p. 40.}}}}

{{smaller block|I hire ''touched'' swiche tales as me told were.
{{right|''William of Palerne'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 4108.}}}}

{{smaller block|For they be as skilful in picking, rifling, and filching as the upright men, and nothing inferior to them in all kind of wickedness, as in other places hereafter they shall be ''touched''. {{right|''Harman'', Caveat for Cursetors, p. 21.}}}}
